-- title:
--   Pull-back of an invertible ideal sheaf to an integral scheme
-- statement:
--   Let $X$ and $Y$ be schemes, with $X$ integral, and let $I$ be an ideal sheaf datum on $Y$ (a `Scheme.IdealSheafData`). Assume $I$ is invertible in the project's sense: for every point $y$ of $Y$ there are an affine open $U \subseteq Y$ and a section $f \in \Gamma(Y, U)$ with $y \in Y.\mathrm{basicOpen}\, f$, together with an element $s$ of $\Gamma(Y, Y.\mathrm{affineBasicOpen}\, f)$ that is a non-zero-divisor and satisfies $I.\mathrm{ideal}\,(Y.\mathrm{affineBasicOpen}\, f) = (s)$. Let $g : X \to Y$ be a morphism of schemes, and suppose that the support of the inverse-image ideal sheaf $I.\mathrm{comap}\, g$, as a subset of $X$, is not the whole of $X$. The conclusion is that $I.\mathrm{comap}\, g$ is again invertible in that same sense: every point $x$ of $X$ lies in a basic open $X.\mathrm{basicOpen}\, f'$ for some affine open $V \subseteq X$ and $f' \in \Gamma(X, V)$ such that the ideal cut out by $I.\mathrm{comap}\, g$ on $X.\mathrm{affineBasicOpen}\, f'$ is generated by a single non-zero-divisor.
--
--   This is the standard criterion for the pull-back of an effective Cartier divisor along a morphism from an integral scheme to be again an effective Cartier divisor: the local equation stays a non-zero-divisor as soon as the image of $X$ is not contained in the divisor. It is used in the treatment of relative Cartier divisors on modular curves and in the resolution of crossing quotients, where pull-backs of divisors along chart morphisms must be seen to remain invertible.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_AlgebraicGeometry_Scheme_IdealSheafData_IsInvertible_comap_of_isIntegral.lean

